    "teacher_markdown": "### **Teacher Tips**\r\n\r\n**Students may focus on the values and overlook the property names**\r\n\r\nStudents often pay attention to the generated values (\"blue\", \"Nike\", \"25\") without noticing that the property names describe what each value means. Encourage them to identify both parts by asking: **\"Which part is the label, and which part is the value?\"**\r\n\r\nRelevant code:\r\n```javascript\r\nconst backpack = {\r\n  brand: \"Nike\",\r\n  color: \"blue\",\r\n  capacity: 25,\r\n  hasLaptopCompartment: true,\r\n  weight: 1.2\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\nHelp students recognize that:\r\n- `brand`, `color`, `capacity` are property names.\r\n- `\"Nike\"`, `\"blue\"`, `25` are values.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**Students may think changing a value means changing the property name**\r\n\r\nWhen asked to modify the object, some students may rename properties instead of updating the values. Encourage them to compare before and after by asking: **\"Did you change what the object knows, or did you change what the information is called?\"**\r\n\r\nRelevant code:\r\n```javascript\r\ncolor: \"blue\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nPossible update:\r\n```javascript\r\ncolor: \"red\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis helps students distinguish between a property and the value stored in it.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**Students may accept the AI-generated object without checking it against their plan**\r\n\r\nStudents may copy the AI-generated object directly into their code without verifying that it matches the warm-up list they created. Encourage them to review the object by asking: **\"Do the property names match the information you planned to include?\"**\r\n\r\nRelevant code:\r\n```javascript\r\nconst headphones = {\r\n  brand: \"Beats\",\r\n  wireless: true,\r\n  color: \"red\",\r\n  batteryLife: 30,\r\n  noiseCancelling: true\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\nRemind students that AI should help generate the object, but they are responsible for ensuring it reflects their intended design."
  },
